I synced SwaggerHub to our repository to auto generate client SDKs. Meaning, it's not necessary to manually create layers like authController to interact with the DB. That part is automatic.

It would look something like this:
```js
import { AuthenticationApi } from './backend/swaggerhub/api';

const authApi = new AuthenticationApi('https://your-api-url.com');

// Example: User login endpoint
app.post('/api/login', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const result = await authApi.authLoginPost({
      email: req.body.email,
      password: req.body.password
    });
    res.json({ token: result.body.token });
  } catch (error) {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication failed' });
  }
});
```
The main benefit of this is that you don't have to do the same work in two places. You no longer have to make sure your API code is written exactly the same as swaggerhub config.  The work you do on SwaggerHub becomes the source of truth and all you have to do for the backend is to use the generated code to fit the endpoints.

